## 5. Rates, value ranges and counter (60 s window)

Illustrative figures from the demo data. Reproduce yours with
`candump -L -n 4000 vcan0 > /tmp/run.log` and the snippet in README section 4, or with `python python/check_data.py`.

| CAN ID | ECU | Frames in 60 s | Expected | Avg gap (ms) | Min / max gap (ms) | Value range seen | Contract range | Counter gaps |
|--------|-----|---------------|----------|--------------|--------------------|------------------|----------------|--------------|
| `0x100` | RPM | 6000 | 6000 | 10.00 | 9.01 / 10.99 | 800 to 6000 | 800 to 6000 | 0 |
| `0x200` | Speed | 3001 | 3000 | 20.00 | 19.02 / 20.97 | 0 to 180 | 0 to 180 | 0 |
| `0x300` | Steering | 1200 | 1200 | 50.00 | 49.03 / 50.98 | 92 to 906 | 0 to 1000 | 0 |
| `0x400` | Temperature | 600 | 600 | 100.00 | 99.04 / 100.94 | 719 to 852 | 700 to 1100 | 0 |

A counter gap of 0 means byte 2 went up by exactly 1 on every frame of that ID, so no frame was lost on the wire.

